the word   'café'   utf-8 63 61 66 c3 a9   latin-1 63 61 66 e9

   cafÃ©        5 chars   utf-8 read as latin-1
                0063 0061 0066 00C3 00A9
   cafÃ©        5 chars   utf-8 read as cp1252
                0063 0061 0066 00C3 00A9
   caf├⌐        5 chars   utf-8 read as cp437
                0063 0061 0066 251C 2310
   cafÃÂ©      7 chars   double encoded
                0063 0061 0066 00C3 0083 00C2 00A9
   caf�         4 chars   latin-1 read as utf-8
                0063 0061 0066 FFFD

THE TELLS
   Ã  before the accented letter  -> utf-8 read as a Latin-1-ish table.
      c3 is A-tilde in every one of them, so the leading character is
      almost always Ã and the SECOND character names the table.
   Ã© vs Ã©  -> Latin-1 and cp1252 differ only above 0x7F, and a9 is
      the same in both here; change the letter and they separate.
   Box- and line-drawing characters -- caf|- and the like -> a DOS code
      page such as cp437, whose top half is not letters at all.
   SEVEN characters where the single-pass cases have five -- four in
      place of the letter instead of two, one of them
      U+0083, a C1 control that draws as nothing -> DOUBLE ENCODED. The
      glyphs alone will not tell you; the length and the code points
      will. Each pass through the wrong table roughly doubles the byte
      count and drags in characters nobody can see.
   U+FFFD  -> the opposite direction. Latin-1 bytes read as UTF-8 do not
      form valid sequences, so a strict decoder RAISES and a lenient one
      substitutes. This is the only case in the list that a program can
      notice on its own.

WHICH ONES ARE REPAIRABLE
   'cafÃ©'.encode('latin-1').decode('utf-8') -> 'café'
   Latin-1 is TOTAL -- every byte 00..FF has a character -- so the trip
   through it loses nothing and reverses exactly.

   cp1252 is not total: five byte values are unassigned. If the text
   passed through one of them the information is gone, and the repair
   raises instead of quietly returning something wrong -- which is the
   behaviour you want at that point.

   And the replacement characters are not repairable at all. U+FFFD is
   not a record of what was there; it is a record that something was.
